ConversionFunctionnumber -&gt; string[nombre.toString()], a template such as ["$nombre"], ["" + nombre], or a format such as ["%d".format(nombre)]string -&gt; int[chaine.toInt()], [chaine.toIntOrNull()]string -&gt; Long[chaine.toLong()], [chaine.toLongOrNull()]string -&gt; Double[chaine.toDouble()], [chaine.toDoubleOrNull()]string -&gt; Float[chaine.toFloat()], [chaine.toFloatOrNull()]string -&gt; BigDecimal[chaine.toBigDecimal()], [chaine.toBigDecimalOrNull()]string -&gt; Boolean[chaine.toBoolean()], [chaine.toBooleanStrictOrNull()] <p>These conversion functions are part of the Kotlin library (package [kotlin.text]) and are called directly on the string itself: ["10".toInt()] rather than the Java function [Integer.parseInt("10")], which remains usable. These are extension functions: functions written outside the [String] class, but called just like its methods. We’ll learn how to write them in Chapter 4.</p> <p>Converting a string to a number may fail if the string does not represent a valid number. A function like [toInt()] then generates an error called an exception—in this case, of type [NumberFormatException]—which terminates the program if it is not handled. This error can be handled by the following [try / catch] clause:</p> <pre><code>try {
    appel de la fonction susceptible de générer l'exception
} catch (e: NumberFormatException) {
    traiter l'exception e
}
instruction suivante
</code></pre> <p>If the function does not throw an exception, execution proceeds to the next statement; otherwise, it proceeds to the body of the [catch] clause, then to the next statement. We will revisit exception handling in the section dedicated to it.</p> <p>Kotlin also provides, for each conversion, a version that does not throw an exception: [toIntOrNull()], [toDoubleOrNull()]... return [null] if the conversion fails. Their return values are nullable ([Int?], [Double?]...): the compiler therefore requires that the failure be handled. When combined with the Elvis operator, they allow you to write a conversion with a default value in a single line: [saisie.toIntOrNull() ?: 0]. Java has no equivalent; the Java course wrote its own method, which returned a [OptionalInt].</p> <p>These functions accept a leading + or - sign, but no spaces or thousand separators: use the function [trim()] to remove leading and trailing spaces from an input before converting it.</p> <p>The program [Bases07.kt] demonstrates several techniques for converting between numbers and strings. The function [affiche] displays the value of its parameter on the screen:</p> <pre><code>// conversions between numbers and strings
package bases07

import java.text.NumberFormat
import java.util.Locale

fun main() {
     // data
    val i = 10
    val l = 100000L
    val f = 45.78F
    val d = -14.98

     // number --&gt; string
    affiche(i.toString())
    affiche(l.toString())
    affiche("$f")
    affiche("" + d)

     // boolean --&gt; string
    val b = false
    affiche(b.toString())

     // string --&gt; int
    var i1 = "10".toInt()
    affiche(i1.toString())
    try {
        i1 = "10.67".toInt()
        affiche(i1.toString())
    } catch (e: NumberFormatException) {
        affiche("Erreur : ${e.message}")
    }

     // string --&gt; long
    var l1 = "100".toLong()
    affiche(l1.toString())
    try {
        l1 = "10.675".toLong()
        affiche(l1.toString())
    } catch (e: NumberFormatException) {
        affiche("Erreur : ${e.message}")
    }

     // string --&gt; Double: the decimal separator is always a period
    var d1 = "100.87".toDouble()
    affiche(d1.toString())
    try {
        d1 = "abcd".toDouble()
        affiche(d1.toString())
    } catch (e: NumberFormatException) {
        affiche("Erreur : ${e.message}")
    }

     // string --&gt; Float
    var f1 = "100.87".toFloat()
    affiche(f1.toString())
    try {
        f1 = "100,87".toFloat()
        affiche(f1.toString())
    } catch (e: NumberFormatException) {
        affiche("Erreur : ${e.message}")
    }

     // string --&gt; BigDecimal
    val m1 = "100.87".toBigDecimal()
    affiche(m1.toString())

     // string --&gt; Boolean
    affiche("${"true".toBoolean()} ${"oui".toBoolean()} ${"oui".toBooleanStrictOrNull()}")

     // Conversions without exceptions: the xxxOrNull functions return null on failure
    val i2 = "123".toIntOrNull()
    if (i2 != null) {
        affiche("\"123\".toIntOrNull() a réussi : $i2")
    }
    val i3 = "12x".toIntOrNull()
    if (i3 == null) {
        affiche("\"12x\".toIntOrNull() a échoué")
    }
    affiche("avec une valeur par défaut : ${"12x".toIntOrNull() ?: -1}")
     // Spaces are not allowed: they are removed using `trim()`
    affiche("[ 42 ] : ${" 42 ".toIntOrNull()}, ${" 42 ".trim().toIntOrNull()}")

     // The user’s language (the “locale”) only affects the formats
    affiche("Locale courante : ${Locale.getDefault().toLanguageTag()}")
    affiche("%.2f".format(d1))
    affiche("%.2f".format(Locale.ROOT, d1))
     // Reading a number written in the French style: the Java class NumberFormat
     // parse may throw a ParseException: Kotlin does not require you to handle it
    val nombre = NumberFormat.getInstance(Locale.FRANCE).parse("100,87")
    affiche("NumberFormat : ${nombre.toDouble()}")
}

// A function that prints its parameter
fun affiche(s: String) {
    println("S=$s")
}
</code></pre> <p>Results:</p> <pre><code>&gt; gradlew -q run -Pmain=bases07.Bases07Kt
